tly, I have one W1 in hand.\n\nAnd one has been discarded (by me).\n\nSo three remain in unshown.\n\nSo W1 is still relatively safe.\n\nBut since I already discarded one and no one claimed, likely not critical.\n\nBut to be safe, better to discard a tile that hasn\u2019t been played.\n\nBut all tiles are public.\n\nAfter careful evaluation, the safest and most logical discard is **T7**.\n\nWhy? Because:\n\n- It is a middle tile not adjacent to any exposed chow by opponents.\n- Across player has T4-T5-T6 \u2014 so T7 is next door, but not overlapping.\n- T7-T8-T9 is possible, but no T8/T9 activity.\n- I have no T6 or T8, so cannot form chow.\n- T7 is less likely to complete a hand than terminals or dragons.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Play J3**? I have two. Can I discard one?\n\nNo \u2014 J3 is my best shot at Dragon Pung (2 points). With only one J3 played, two remain. High incentive to keep.\n\nMust keep both J3s.\n\nFinal choice: **Play T7**\n\nIt is a clean discard, not supporting any obvious hand, and not connecting to my own fragments.\n\nReason: T7 is an isolated middle tile with no immediate chow potential in hand and no strong signals from opponents indicating its value. Discarding it minimizes risk while simplifying the hand toward honor-based or random-chance improvement.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
